---
description: Require JSDoc purpose, @param, and @returns on every function in src TypeScript
globs: src/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

# JSDoc on every function

Every function in `src/**/*.{ts,tsx}` needs a JSDoc block immediately above it. The block must describe the function's purpose: what it does and why it exists. Do not restate the function name.

Document every parameter with a `@param` tag. Describe what the argument is used for. TypeScript already carries the type, so do not repeat it. For a destructured parameter, document each property. Omit `@param` only when the function takes no arguments.

Leave one blank line between the purpose description and the tag list. The list starts with `@param` tags, then `@returns`. When the function takes no arguments, the blank line still separates the description from `@returns`. Describe what the caller receives. Omit `@returns` only when the function returns nothing.

Apply this to:

- Function declarations and function expressions
- Arrow functions bound to a variable, export, or property
- Class and object methods
- React function components and custom hooks

Skip one-line callbacks passed inline (for example `.map((item) => item.id)`) unless the callback is extracted into a named function.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — missing JSDoc
export function searchCandidates(query: string) {
  return db.candidate.findMany({ where: { name: { contains: query } } });
}

// ❌ BAD — no blank line, parameters and return undocumented
/**
 * Finds discoverable candidates whose name matches the recruiter's query.
 * @param query Text to match against the candidate's name.
 */
export function searchCandidates(query: string, limit: number) {
  return db.candidate.findMany({
    where: { name: { contains: query } },
    take: limit,
  });
}

// ✅ GOOD
/**
 * Finds discoverable candidates whose name matches the recruiter's query.
 *
 * @param query Text to match against the candidate's name.
 * @param limit Maximum number of candidates to return.
 * @returns Matching candidates, capped at limit.
 */
export function searchCandidates(query: string, limit: number) {
  return db.candidate.findMany({
    where: { name: { contains: query } },
    take: limit,
  });
}
```

When writing or editing a function, add or update the JSDoc in the same change. If behavior, parameters, or the return value change, update the purpose description, `@param` tags, and `@returns` tag so they stay accurate.
